          "question": "What is the symptoms and abnormal findings chapter for, and when should a code from it be used instead of a disease code?",
          "answer": "The \"Symptoms and Abnormal Findings\" chapter in the ICD-10 coding system is primarily used for conditions where a definitive diagnosis has not yet been established. This chapter includes a wide range of symptoms and abnormal clinical findings that may require further investigation. According to the ICD-10 guidelines, a code from this chapter should be used when a patient presents with a symptom or abnormal finding that cannot be directly associated with a confirmed disease process.\n\nYou would select a code from this chapter instead of a disease code when the clinical situation involves a sign or symptom without a corresponding definitive diagnosis. This is particularly relevant in cases where additional diagnostic workup is needed to determine the underlying condition.\n\nI could not cover specific examples of symptoms or codes within this chapter or provide guidance on particular clinical scenarios beyond what the coding guidelines state.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
